WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.860
This lake stickleback has almost no pelvis, yet its Pitx1 protein is unchanged.

2
00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:12.220
So what broke, if the protein did not?

3
00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.460
The likely answer is a kind of switch.

4
00:00:16.583 --> 00:00:21.703
Marine sticklebacks carry a pelvis: a bony girdle with a pair of long spines.

5
00:00:22.903 --> 00:00:25.283
It is the fish's version of your hips and legs.

6
00:00:26.643 --> 00:00:31.483
In Paxton Lake, British Columbia, the bottom-feeding fish have a greatly reduced

7
00:00:31.483 --> 00:00:35.143
pelvis. Most have no pelvic bones at all.

8
00:00:36.117 --> 00:00:39.857
The gene most strongly tied to that difference is Pitx1.

9
00:00:41.197 --> 00:00:43.437
Its protein is a transcription factor.

10
00:00:44.637 --> 00:00:47.877
It binds DNA and switches other genes on or off.

11
00:00:49.137 --> 00:00:52.017
In the lake fish examined, that protein was unchanged.

12
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.820
Pitx1 is not only a pelvis gene.

13
00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:58.580
It is also used in the jaw.

14
00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:04.900
Mice with no working Pitx1 die, and they have malformed hind limbs among other

15
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:05.900
defects.

16
00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:09.370
Suppose the lake fish had broken the protein itself.

17
00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:12.550
Where would you expect the damage to show?

18
00:01:19.067 --> 00:01:20.667
Everywhere the gene works.

19
00:01:21.867 --> 00:01:26.067
A protein with no function would fail in the jaw and the pelvis at once.

20
00:01:27.247 --> 00:01:29.407
The mouse shows how costly that can be.

21
00:01:30.367 --> 00:01:32.087
The lake fish do not look like that.

22
00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.380
The way out is an enhancer.

23
00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:41.960
An enhancer is a short stretch of noncoding DNA that switches a gene on in

24
00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:42.960
particular tissues.

25
00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:45.600
One gene can have several.

26
00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:49.240
One can serve the jaw, another the pelvis.

27
00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:51.740
Their jobs can overlap.

28
00:01:52.833 --> 00:01:55.973
So where is Pitx1 RNA in a lake fish larva?

29
00:01:57.233 --> 00:02:01.713
A labelled probe pairs with the RNA and leaves a gold mark wherever it is.

30
00:02:02.873 --> 00:02:06.053
In Paxton larvae, gold sits in the mouth and lower jaw.

31
00:02:07.313 --> 00:02:09.093
Where the pelvis forms, there is none.

32
00:02:10.167 --> 00:02:14.267
So suppose the jaw switch still works, but the pelvic switch does not.

33
00:02:15.567 --> 00:02:19.067
Then that body part shrinks, while the gene keeps its other jobs.

34
00:02:20.167 --> 00:02:22.167
Compare the two fish once more.

35
00:02:23.367 --> 00:02:25.427
Both make the same Pitx1 protein.

36
00:02:26.787 --> 00:02:27.927
Both use it in the jaw.

37
00:02:29.207 --> 00:02:32.087
Of the tissues compared, only the pelvic use differs.

38
00:02:33.427 --> 00:02:36.167
The pelvic use changed, and the jaw use was spared.

39
00:02:37.527 --> 00:02:39.047
Other tissues were not all checked.

40
00:02:40.117 --> 00:02:44.157
This is the best-studied route to a reduced pelvis in sticklebacks.

41
00:02:45.417 --> 00:02:46.797
It is not the only one.

42
00:02:47.917 --> 00:02:50.677
A missing gold mark also leaves a question open.

43
00:02:51.917 --> 00:02:55.237
Is the break in this gene's own DNA, or in another gene?
